// @expect-error A 'char' is a code point rather than a quantity, so it has no 'float' form of its own. Write 'as float' where the code point's number is what you mean.
// The narrower floating-point type is refused for the same reason as the wider one, and a 'char'
// variable is refused exactly as a literal is.
main() -> int {
    char letter = 'A';
    float code = letter;
    return code > 0.0 ? 0 : 1;
}
